# The initramfs overlay

Whatever is in this directory is merged into the early userspace the kernel
unpacks: the *contents* map to that archive's root, so `core/init` becomes
`/init` and replaces what the packages put there. Directories are merged; files
and symlinks overwrite.

It is merged before the machine's own tree, and after everything generated from
`initramfs.toml`, so a file here is the authority over anything the build writes
- and a machine may still replace anything here.

## Core and machine trees

This tree is the **shared** half of both machines' archives: one copy of each
file, installed into the x86_64 archive and the aarch64 archive alike. The
per-machine trees (`initramfs/x86_64/`, `initramfs/aarch64/`) merge *after* it.
Both are **empty today** and that is a state, not an oversight: the `/init`
script is one file for both machines (see below), and a machine's own early
userspace has had nothing else to add. The place to put a file both machines
want is this one; a file only one machine's early userspace wants goes in its
own tree, whose directory a merge creates on demand.

The disk's userland has the same split (`assets/rootfs/core/`), and the reason is
the same one: one copy of what both machines want, so that two copies cannot
drift. It has happened here - this was two `/init` scripts, the second one's
header claiming to be the first one while the bodies differed by the selftest
probe, the switch-root verdict line and the name of the disk's own pid-1 chain.

## init

`init` is the script the kernel runs as pid 1 on the ramfs, on both machines. It
mounts the pseudo-filesystems, mounts the filesystem the command line names with
`root=`, moves the mounts onto it and `switch_root`s into the disk's own pid-1
chain - `/init` if the disk ships one, `/sbin/init` otherwise, which is the one
machine difference the script has left and the disk itself answers it.

It lives here rather than in a Rust string in `xtask`, so editing it is editing
a shell script. It must stay executable - the kernel execs it - and the archive
carries the mode it has in this tree.

Three things are worth knowing when changing it:

* Anything created *before* `switch_root` disappears with the ramfs; anything
  mounted onto the new root survives. That is why the script makes `/sysroot/tmp`
  and friends and then mounts onto them.
* `mount -t devpts devpts /sysroot/dev/pts` is not optional. Without it every
  `openpty` in the real system fails with ENOENT - which is how a `doas` or a
  nested shell reports "unable to allocate pty".
* The applets it execs are a contract with the applet farm: the build reads the
  `@vocabulary:` line at the end of the script and `cargo test -p mpos-xtask`
  fails when a verb on it is not an applet the archive carries. A name missing
  from the farm is a boot that dies on ENOENT mid-pivot, and a headless verdict
  reads that as a hang.

## An extra mount at boot

```sh
# initramfs/core/etc/extra-mount.sh
#!/bin/sh
mount -t tmpfs tmpfs /tmp
```
